{:edb/db-before-t 2584 :edb/db-after-t 2585 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535897 50 #inst "2026-10-09T10:08:55.669Z" 13194139535897 true] [17592186228425 1104 #edb/ref 17592186228426 13194139535897 true] [17592186228431 1104 #edb/ref 17592186228434 13194139535897 true] [17592186228435 1104 #edb/ref 17592186228436 13194139535897 true] [17592186228457 1000 #edb/ref 17592186373201 13194139535897 true] [17592186228457 1104 #edb/ref 17592186373201 13194139535897 true] [17592186228886 1100 "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$$\ny'' - 2y' - 3y = 0,  \\qquad y'(1) = y'(3) = 0,\\quad 1\\le x\\le3\n$$\n\nLet $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ be the fundamental solutions, and suppose we define the matrix $A$ as \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067309/assets/aa58b04d0ebdd18e4259fd3a1386332043f317f12b94c7b4ad468bef77584a0f.png)\n\nFind the fundamental solutions and use $A$ to determine if the given BVP has a unique or infinitely many solutions." 13194139535897 true] [17592186228886 1100 "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$y'' - 2y' - 3y = 0, \\qquad y'(1) = y'(3) = 0,\\quad 1\\le x\\le3$\n\nLet $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ be the fundamental solutions, and suppose we define the matrix $A$ as\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}y_{1}^{′}(1) & y_{2}^{′}(1) \\\\ y_{1}^{′}(3) & y_{2}^{′}(3)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFind the fundamental solutions and use $A$ to determine if the given BVP has a unique or infinitely many solutions." 13194139535897 false] [17592186228886 1145 "Suppose we have the second-order homogeneous boundary value (BVP) problem\n\n$$\ny''(x)+P(x)y'(x)+Q(x)y=0,\\qquad a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwith the Robin (mixed) boundary conditions\n\n$$\n\\alpha_{1}y(a)+\\beta_{1}y'(a)=0,\\qquad\n\\alpha_{2}y(b)+\\beta_{2}y'(b)=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ and $Q$ are continuous on $[a,b],$ and $\\alpha_i, \\beta_i$ are real constants such that $(\\alpha_i,\\beta_i)\\ne(0,0).$ Furthermore, suppose the fundamental solutions to the equation are $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x).$  \n\nNow, let's define the following matrix:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067309/assets/ac7be07071df654f4d2fec18b6b93afb21fd13169e7890ea091b496d7f9b206c.png)\n\nThen, the BVP has a unique, trivial solution $y\\equiv0$ if and only if $\\det(A)\\ne0.$\nIn other words, if the determinant equals zero, then the BVP has *infinitely many solutions*.\n\nNotice that, in our case, we have Neumann boundary conditions.  So, setting $\\alpha_i=0,$ $\\beta_i=1,$ $a=1,$ and $b=3,$ the matrix $A$ reduces to\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067309/assets/91f85685d5a17221398842923709c20b0e296e53fedb30ee6a4fdcb16d99e0d8.png)\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y=e^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-2m-3 & =0 \\\\ (m-3)(m+1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=3$ and $m=-1,$ and so the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\ny_{1}(x)=e^{3x}\\;\\textrm{and}\\;y_{2}(x)=e^{-x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\ny_1'(x) = 3e^{3x}, \\qquad y_2'(x) = -e^{-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix $A$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{3(1)} & -{e}^{-1} \\\\ 3{e}^{3(3)} & -{e}^{-3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{3} & -{e}^{-1} \\\\ 3{e}^{9} & -{e}^{-3}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =(3{e}^{3})(-{e}^{-3})-(-{e}^{-1})(3{e}^{9}) \\\\  & =-3+3{e}^{8} \\\\  & =3({e}^{8}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\det(A)\\ne0,$ we conclude that this BVP has the unique trivial solution $y\\equiv0.$" 13194139535897 true] [17592186228886 1145 "Suppose we have the second-order homogeneous boundary value (BVP) problem\n\n$$\ny''(x)+P(x)y'(x)+Q(x)y=0,\\qquad a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwith the Robin (mixed) boundary conditions\n\n$$\n\\alpha_{1}y(a)+\\beta_{1}y'(a)=0,\\qquad \\alpha_{2}y(b)+\\beta_{2}y'(b)=0\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ and $Q$ are continuous on $[a,b]$, and $\\alpha_i, \\beta_i$ are real constants such that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha_i,\\beta_i)\\ne(0,0)\n$$\n\nFurthermore, suppose the fundamental solutions to the equation are $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$.\n\nNow, let's define the following matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}α_{1}y_{1}(a) + β_{1}y_{1}^{′}(a) & α_{1}y_{2}(a) + β_{1}y_{2}^{′}(a) \\\\ α_{2}y_{1}(b) + β_{2}y_{1}^{′}(b) & α_{2}y_{2}(b) + β_{2}y_{2}^{′}(b)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, the BVP has a unique, trivial solution $y\\equiv0$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\det(A)\\ne0\n$$\n\nIn other words, if the determinant equals zero, then the BVP has *infinitely many solutions*.\n\nNotice that, in our case, we have Neumann boundary conditions. So, setting $\\alpha_i=0$, $\\beta_i=1$, $a=1$, and $b=3$, the matrix $A$ reduces to\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}y_{1}^{′}(1) & y_{2}^{′}(1) \\\\ y_{1}^{′}(3) & y_{2}^{′}(3)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y=e^{mx}$. The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm^{2} - 2m - 3 &= 0 \\\\\n(m - 3)(m + 1) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=3$ and $m=-1$, and so the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\ny_{1}(x)=e^{3x}\\;\\textrm{and}\\;y_{2}(x)=e^{-x}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\ny_1'(x) = 3e^{3x}, \\qquad y_2'(x) = -e^{-x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix $A$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}3e^{3(1)} & -e^{-1} \\\\ 3e^{3(3)} & -e^{-3}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3e^{3} & -e^{-1} \\\\ 3e^{9} & -e^{-3}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndet(A) &= (3e^{3})(-e^{-3}) - (-e^{-1})(3e^{9}) \\\\\n&=-3 + 3e^{8} \\\\\n&= 3 (e 8 - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\det(A)\\ne0\n$$\n\nwe conclude that this BVP has the unique trivial solution $y\\equiv0$." 13194139535897 false] [17592186228888 1100 "Consider the following boundary value problem.\n\n$$\ny'' = 0, \\qquad y(4) = 0, \\quad y(3) + y'(3) = 0,\\quad 3\\leq x \\leq 4\n$$\n\nFind the solution to this boundary value problem in terms of an arbitrary constant $A.$" 13194139535897 true] [17592186228888 1100 "Consider the following boundary value problem.\n\n$y'' = 0, \\qquad y(4) = 0, \\quad y(3) + y'(3) = 0,\\quad 3\\leq x \\leq 4$\n\nFind the solution to this boundary value problem in terms of an arbitrary constant $A$." 13194139535897 false] [17592186228888 1145 "To solve the homogeneous boundary value problem, we first find the general solution and then determine the values of the arbitrary constants that fit the boundary conditions.\n\nWe have the equation\n\n$$\ny''(x) = 0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation twice, we get the general solution\n\n$$\ny = Ax + B.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\ny' = A.\n$$\n\nApplying the condition $y(4) = 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n4A + B = 0 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad B = -4A.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\ny = Ax - 4A = A(x-4).\n$$\n\nApplying the condition $y(3) + y'(3) = 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n-A+A = 0,\n$$\n\nwhich is automatically satisfied for any $A.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the BVP is $y = A\\cdot \\left(x-4\\right),$\nwhere $A$ is arbitrary." 13194139535897 true] [17592186228888 1145 "To solve the homogeneous boundary value problem, we first find the general solution and then determine the values of the arbitrary constants that fit the boundary conditions.\n\nWe have the equation\n$y''(x) = 0$.\n\nIntegrating this equation twice, we get the general solution\n\n$$\ny = Ax + B\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n$y' = A$.\n\nApplying the condition $y(4) = 0$, we have\n\n$$\n4A + B = 0 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad B = -4A\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\ny = Ax - 4A = A(x-4)\n$$\n\nApplying the condition\n\n$$\ny(3) + y'(3) = 0\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n-A+A = 0\n$$\n\nwhich is automatically satisfied for any $A$.\n\nTherefore, the solution to the BVP is\n\n$$\ny = A\\cdot \\left(x-4\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is arbitrary." 13194139535897 false] [17592186229104 1020 #edb/ref 17592186373209 13194139535897 true] [17592186229104 1020 #edb/ref 17592186373228 13194139535897 true] [17592186229104 1020 #edb/ref 17592186373247 13194139535897 true] [17592186229104 1020 #edb/ref 17592186373265 13194139535897 true] [17592186229105 1020 #edb/ref 17592186373202 13194139535897 true] [17592186229105 1020 #edb/ref 17592186373206 13194139535897 true] [17592186229105 1020 #edb/ref 17592186373284 13194139535897 true] [17592186229105 1020 #edb/ref 17592186373287 13194139535897 true] [17592186229667 1017 #edb/ref 17592186228420 13194139535897 false] [17592186229667 1017 #edb/ref 17592186373196 13194139535897 true] [17592186229667 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535897 true] [17592186229667 1100 "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}-3y=0,\\,y(0)=y(1)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 1\n$$\n\nLet ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ be the fundamental solutions, and suppose we define the matrix $A$ as \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(0) & {y}_{2}(0) \\\\ {y}_{1}(1) & {y}_{2}(1)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFill in the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$ The boundary value problem has a unique solution if and only if {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The fundamental solutions to this BVP are {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ For this BVP, we have $det(A)=$  {{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, this BVP {{field-4}}." 13194139535897 true] [17592186229667 1100 "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$y^{″} - 2y^{′} - 3y = 0, y(0) = y(1) = 0, 0 \\le x \\le 1$\n\nLet $y_{1}(x)$ and $y_{2}(x)$ be the fundamental solutions, and suppose we define the matrix $A$ as\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}y_{1}(0) & y_{2}(0) \\\\ y_{1}(1) & y_{2}(1)\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nFill in the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe boundary value problem has a unique solution if and only if {{field-1}}.\n\nThe fundamental solutions to this BVP are {{field-2}}.\n\nFor this BVP, we have $det(A) =$ {{field-3}}.\n\nTherefore, this BVP {{field-4}}." 13194139535897 false] [17592186229667 1145 "Suppose we have the second-order homogeneous boundary value (BVP) problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\\,a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwith the Robin (mixed) boundary conditions\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}y(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}^{′}(a)=0,\\,{\\alpha }_{2}y(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}^{′}(b)=0\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ and $Q$ are continuous on $[a,b],$ and ${\\alpha }_{i}$ and ${\\beta }_{i}$ are real constants such that $({\\alpha }_{i},{\\beta }_{i})\\ne (0,0).$ Furthermore, suppose the fundamental solutions to the equation are ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ \n\nNow, let's define the following matrix:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}\\,{\\alpha }_{1}{y}_{1}(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}_{1}^{′}(a) & {\\alpha }_{1}{y}_{2}(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}(a) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{2}{y}_{1}(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}(b) & {\\alpha }_{2}{y}_{2}(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}_{2}^{′}(b)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, the BVP has a unique, trivial solution $y≡0$ if and only if $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nIn other words, if the determinant equals zero, then the BVP has *infinitely many solutions*.\n\nNotice that, in our case, we have Dirichlet boundary conditions. So, setting ${\\alpha }_{i}=1,{\\beta }_{i}=0,a=0,$ and $b=1,$ the matrix $A$ reduces to\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(0) & {y}_{2}(0) \\\\ {y}_{1}(1) & {y}_{2}(1)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-2m-3 & =0 \\\\ (m-3)(m+1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=3$ and $m=-1,$ and so the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x}\\text{ and }{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix $A$ is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ {e}^{3} & {e}^{-1}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & ={e}^{-1}-{e}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{e}-{e}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{4}}{e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(A)\\ne 0$ we conclude that this BVP $\\text{has the unique solution }y≡0.$" 13194139535897 true] [17592186229670 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535897 true] [17592186229670 1100 "Consider the following boundary value problem.\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0,\\,y(-2)=0,\\,y(2)-4{y}^{′}(2)=0,\\,-2\\le x\\le 2\n$$\n\nFind the solution to this boundary value problem in terms of an arbitrary constant $A.$\n\nThe solution is $y=A\\,\\cdot ($\n{{field-1}}$).$" 13194139535897 true] [17592186229670 1100 "Consider the following boundary value problem.\n\n$y^{″} = 0, y(-2) = 0, y(2) - 4y^{′}(2) = 0,-2 \\le x \\le 2$\nFind the solution to this boundary value problem in terms of an arbitrary constant $A$.\n\nThe solution is $y = A \\cdot ($\n{{field-1}} $)$." 13194139535897 false] [17592186229670 1145 "To solve the homogeneous boundary value problem, we first find the general solution and then determine the values of the arbitrary constants that fit the boundary conditions.\n\nWe have the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation twice, we get the general solution\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=A.\n$$\n\nApplying the condition $y(-2)=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n-2A+B=0\\,⇒\\,B=2A.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\ny=Ax+2A=A(x+2).\n$$\n\nApplying the condition $y(2)-4{y}^{′}(2)=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n4A-4A=0\n$$\n\nwhich is automatically satisfied for any $A.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the BVP is $y=A\\cdot (x+2),$ where $A$ is arbitrary." 13194139535897 true] [17592186373196 1000 #edb/ref 17592186373197 13194139535897 true] [17592186373196 1000 #edb/ref 17592186373198 13194139535897 true] [17592186373196 1000 #edb/ref 17592186373199 13194139535897 true] [17592186373196 1000 #edb/ref 17592186373200 13194139535897 true] [17592186373196 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045451 13194139535897 true] [17592186373196 1041 "field-2" 13194139535897 true] [17592186373196 1104 #edb/ref 17592186373197 13194139535897 true] [17592186373196 1110 #uuid "b9cc9012-ec22-5d99-bbbf-c417d8a79722" 13194139535897 true] [17592186373197 1034 "${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-x}$" 13194139535897 true] [17592186373197 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535897 true] [17592186373197 1111 #uuid "9eb6bc51-a778-51ac-a016-3ffe06a42290" 13194139535897 true] [17592186373198 1034 "${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{2x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-2x}$" 13194139535897 true] [17592186373198 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535897 true] [17592186373198 1111 #uuid "3ee0444d-1993-5b9e-8160-1b5a71a856b5" 13194139535897 true] [17592186373199 1034 "${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$" 13194139535897 true] [17592186373199 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535897 true] [17592186373199 1111 #uuid "b01cc755-bf8e-533d-9b06-6779accc1cda" 13194139535897 true] [17592186373200 1034 "${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$" 13194139535897 true] [17592186373200 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535897 true] [17592186373200 1111 #uuid "b4298bd8-b56a-5198-b598-9ff14d04590b" 13194139535897 true] [17592186373201 1034 "x+2" 13194139535897 true] [17592186373201 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535897 true] [17592186373201 1111 #uuid "199fe473-da76-5426-bfd9-5fc5eac3d897" 13194139535897 true] [17592186373202 1017 #edb/ref 17592186373203 13194139535897 true] [17592186373202 1089 "q-338599" 13194139535897 true] [17592186373202 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535897 true] [17592186373202 1100 "Consider the following boundary value problem.\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=0,\\,y(-4)=0,\\,y(0)-4{y}^{′}(0)=0,\\,-4\\le x\\le 0\n$$\n\nFind the solution to this boundary value problem in terms of an arbitrary constant $A.$\n\nThe solution is $y=A\\,\\cdot ($\n{{field-1}}$).$" 13194139535897 true] [17592186373202 1113 #uuid "f5406da4-e587-583d-979b-bb4ba17a2f3e" 13194139535897 true] [17592186373202 1145 "To solve the homogeneous boundary value problem, we first find the general solution and then determine the values of the arbitrary constants that fit the boundary conditions.\n\nWe have the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation twice, we get the general solution\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=A.\n$$\n\nApplying the condition $y(-4)=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n-4A+B=0\\,⇒\\,B=4A.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\ny=Ax+4A=A(x+4).\n$$\n\nApplying the condition $y(0)-4{y}^{′}(0)=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n4A-4A=0\n$$\n\nwhich is automatically satisfied for any $A.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the BVP is $y=A\\cdot (x+4),$ where $A$ is arbitrary." 13194139535897 true] [17592186373203 1000 #edb/ref 17592186373204 13194139535897 true] [17592186373203 1000 #edb/ref 17592186373205 13194139535897 true] [17592186373203 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535897 true] [17592186373203 1041 "field-1" 13194139535897 true] [17592186373203 1104 #edb/ref 17592186373204 13194139535897 true] [17592186373203 1110 #uuid "b869d149-4c40-554e-8214-fce0ef29583e" 13194139535897 true] [17592186373204 1034 "x+4" 13194139535897 true] [17592186373204 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535897 true] [17592186373204 1111 #uuid "95a4e6b8-51a7-5b47-aff1-0b01772c7583" 13194139535897 true] [17592186373205 1034 "x-4" 13194139535897 true] [17592186373205 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535897 true] [17592186373205 1111 #uuid "6a4311d2-2349-5f81-a1cc-7ed85395c410" 13194139535897 true] [17592186373206 1017 #edb/ref 17592186373207 13194139535897 true] [17592186373206 1089 "q-338645" 13194139535897 true] [17592186373206 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535897 true] [17592186373206 1100 "Consider the following boundary value problem.\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-16y=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)-4y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}\\,(\\frac{1}{2})-4y\\,(\\frac{1}{2})=0,\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to this boundary value problem in terms of an arbitrary constant $A.$\n\nThe solution is $y=A\\,\\cdot $\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535897 true] [17592186373206 1113 #uuid "037aa2da-018b-5f7b-9bc8-1ee3e00b1d17" 13194139535897 true] [17592186373206 1145 "To solve the homogeneous boundary value problem, we first find the general solution and then determine the values of the arbitrary constants that fit the boundary conditions.\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-16 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=\\pm 4.$ So, the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{4x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{4x}+B{e}^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4A{e}^{4x}-4B{e}^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nApplying the condition ${y}^{′}(0)-4y(0)=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4A-4B)-4(A+B) & =0 \\\\ -8B & =0 \\\\ B & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nApplying the condition ${y}^{′}\\,(\\frac{1}{2})-4y\\,(\\frac{1}{2})=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n4A{e}^{2}-4A{e}^{2}=0,\n$$\n\nwhich is automatically satisfied for any $A.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the BVP is $y=A\\cdot {e}^{4x},$ where $A$ is arbitrary." 13194139535897 true] [17592186373207 1000 #edb/ref 17592186373208 13194139535897 true] [17592186373207 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535897 true] [17592186373207 1041 "field-1" 13194139535897 true] [17592186373207 1104 #edb/ref 17592186373208 13194139535897 true] [17592186373207 1110 #uuid "b798a131-4bda-5e02-a47b-66aad8b279e6" 13194139535897 true] [17592186373208 1034 "e^{4x}" 13194139535897 true] [17592186373208 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535897 true] [17592186373208 1111 #uuid "074ddc79-c44b-55e4-8cb5-be16e7cf49e3" 13194139535897 true] [17592186373209 1017 #edb/ref 17592186373210 13194139535897 true] [17592186373209 1017 #edb/ref 17592186373213 13194139535897 true] [17592186373209 1017 #edb/ref 17592186373218 13194139535897 true] [17592186373209 1017 #edb/ref 17592186373224 13194139535897 true] [17592186373209 1089 "q-338686" 13194139535897 true] [17592186373209 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535897 true] [17592186373209 1100 "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-{y}^{′}-2y=0,\\,y(0)=y(1)=0,\\,0\\le x\\le 1\n$$\n\nLet ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ be the fundamental solutions